dsh: reasoning:
Now I have the full card list: 11 base cards + 6 bindchain addenda = 17. Note the bindchain addenda conflict: audit-r2-F1-bindchain-worker-071 pins artifact reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json (addendum writes into the same file), while audit-r2-F1-b pins reviews/F1-review-rev27-b.json. But audit-r2-F1-bindchain-worker-085 also pins reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json — the SAME deliverable path as worker-071's addendum! That's a collision: two different reviewers (071 and 085) told to write the same path `reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json`. That's a genuine finding worth surfacing (deliverable path collision → one reviewer's verdict may overwrite the other's, destroying the independence count).

Wait, let me check: audit-r2-F1-a assignee worker-071 artifact reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json; audit-r2-F1-b assignee worker-085 artifact reviews/F1-review-rev27-b.json; then F1-bindchain-worker-071 artifact reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json (consistent with 071's own file), and F1-bindchain-worker-085 artifact reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json (should be rev27-b!). Let me re-read the output for the bindchain entries:
- audit-r2-F1-bindchain-worker-071 → "artifact": "reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json"
- audit-r2-F1-bindchain-worker-085 → "artifact": "reviews/F1-review-rev27-a.json"  ← collision (should be -b)
